<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Honolulu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Honolulu is the capital and largest city of the US state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, on the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a>. Its sheltered harbour made it a centre for foreign traders from the 1790s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a> made it the capital of the Kingdom of Hawaii in 1845. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iolani-palace">ʻIolani Palace</a> was the seat of the monarchy until its overthrow in 1893, and nearby Pearl Harbor was attacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in December 1941.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/honolulu.jpg" alt="Honolulu" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Farragutful · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2022_Views_from_Diamond_Head_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Honolulu lies along the southeast coast of the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>. The name is Hawaiian for sheltered harbour or calm port, and the old name of the area, Kou, roughly covers the heart of the present downtown district. It is the most remote major city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and one of the most isolated in the world: the nearest point on the mainland, Point Arena Lighthouse in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, is 2,045 nautical miles away, and the nearest major city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>. The volcanic field of the Honolulu Volcanics lies partly within the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Oral histories and artifacts indicate that there was a settlement where Honolulu now stands in the 11th century, founded by the original Polynesian migrants to the archipelago. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nuuanu">Battle of Nuʻuanu</a> at Nuʻuanu Pali, he moved his royal court from the island of Hawaiʻi to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waikiki">Waikiki</a> in 1804, and in 1809 to what is now downtown Honolulu. The capital moved back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kailua-kona">Kailua-Kona</a> in 1812.</p><p>The geography of the area and Honolulu Harbor made it desirable as a port, which accounts for the city's growth and importance in the Hawaiian archipelago and the broader Pacific. Honolulu has been the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> since 1845, first as seat of the independent Hawaiian Kingdom until its overthrow in 1893, then of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> territory established after annexation in 1898, and since 1959 of the state of Hawaii. The city gained worldwide recognition after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> attacked nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a> on 7 December 1941, which brought the United States into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Honolulu is the capital and most populous city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> and the seat of the consolidated City and County of Honolulu. Pearl Harbor remains a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a> base and hosts the United States Pacific Fleet. The Bishop Museum has the world's largest collection of Hawaiiana and Pacific culture artifacts, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iolani-palace">ʻIolani Palace</a> and the USS Arizona Memorial are among the city's sights.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Honolulu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>ʻIolani Palace in <b>Honolulu</b> was lit by electricity by 1887, four years before electric lighting was installed in the White House in Washington in 1891.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>On the ride back to <b>Honolulu</b> the princess began to hum a tune; Charles Wilson remarked that it sounded like &quot;The Lone Rock by the Sea&quot;, and the others took up the humming when the party rested in an orange grove on the Honolulu side of the Pali.</blockquote><a href="/songs/aloha-oe">Aloha ʻOe</a></li><li><blockquote>Earhart took off from <b>Honolulu</b> and landed in Oakland, California, about 18 hours later.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-11">11 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Former Philippine president Ferdinand Marcos died in <b>Honolulu</b>, three years after the People Power Revolution drove him from power.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-28">28 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After a Russian adventure in 1816 to 1817, Kaʻumualiʻi was taken to <b>Honolulu</b> in 1821 and died there in 1824; his son's revolt that year failed.</blockquote><a href="/state/chiefdom-of-kauai">Chiefdom of Kauai</a></li><li><blockquote>Oʻahu became part of the Kingdom of Hawaii, and <b>Honolulu</b> later grew into its capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/chiefdom-of-oahu">Chiefdom of Oahu</a></li></ul></section>
